-- title:
--   Sum final-layer arrays over every terminal graph vertex
-- statement:
--   Suppose the native core invariant holds for the final graph-sketch layer $k$ and a fixed state $a<k$. Let the endpoint index be zero, the endpoint counter contain $n$ markers, and the coefficient accumulator be the zero exterior array. The terminal loop consumes that counter, sets the endpoint index to $n$, and returns the coefficient array of
--   $$
--   G_a=\sum_{v<n}\operatorname{graphLayer}(k,v,a).
--   $$
--   All other state components are preserved. Its charged running time is at most
--   $$
--   (C_{\rm terminal}(n,k)+31)n+30,
--   $$
--   where $C_{\rm terminal}$ is the verified budget for reading and adding one endpoint array and incrementing its index. The theorem supplies the endpoint summation needed before extracting the full-set coefficient in the squared-norm estimator.
-- source:
--   T. Koana, Approximate Counting of k-Paths in O*(2^k) Time, STOC 2027 submission #953, October 2026, §3.2 (printed pp. 8–9 / PDF pp. 9–10) and §4.1 (printed p. 9 / PDF p. 10), https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-2/953.pdf. Native implementation: Solutions/TerminalLoop.lean, line 20; source snapshot SHA-256 a988b33bbd5bb0cb70407197aa1b2da718fcec927e5eef902eea38a7c00efd94.

